    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the daily create limit (429 when 100+ owners are already registered today) by following the existing `RejectCityAtCapacity` idiom, keeping structural impact minimal \u2014 three new small single-method classes (each with `WMC_other = 0`) plus one YAML wiring edit; no existing function was modified.\n\n**New files:**\n- `RejectDailyLimit.java` \u2014 a one-method function that counts owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and throws `DailyLimitExceededException` once the count reaches 100.\n- `DailyLimitExceededException.java` \u2014 a checked exception (per the escalation convention).\n- `DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`.\n- The escalation YAML mapping the exception to its handler.\n\n**Edited:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `rejectDailyLimit` step (under `transaction` governance) into the pipeline before `build`, right after the city-capacity check.\n\nThe step runs before `BuildOwner` assigns the new owner's `registrationDate`, so it counts only pre-existing owners: creating 100 owners today succeeds, and the 101st is rejected with 429.",
